WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.218 --> 00:00:02.179
- The Good Doctor is quickly becoming

00:00:02.179 --> 00:00:04.055
one of my favorite medical dramas,

00:00:04.055 --> 00:00:05.730
and you continually request it.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.480
So, I figured let's just go ahead

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.859
and watch the second
episode of the first season.

00:00:09.859 --> 00:00:13.714
(high pitched noise)

00:00:13.714 --> 00:00:17.112
(subtle music)

00:00:17.112 --> 00:00:17.945
(car engine revs)

00:00:17.945 --> 00:00:19.704
(police sirens blare)

00:00:19.704 --> 00:00:23.244
- That does not look like a
comfortable sleeping position.

00:00:23.244 --> 00:00:27.116
I wish I was this happy when I
woke up early in the morning.

00:00:27.116 --> 00:00:29.636
(triumphal string music)

00:00:29.636 --> 00:00:31.726
What is he measuring?

00:00:31.726 --> 00:00:32.899
- Where to?

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:35.343
- San Jose Saint Bonaventure Hospital.

00:00:35.343 --> 00:00:38.699
I'm a surgical resident,
today's my first full day.

00:00:38.699 --> 00:00:39.532
- I'm that floor.

00:00:39.532 --> 00:00:40.390
(laughs)

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:42.236
- I like this show, I don't
know, it gets me happy,

00:00:42.236 --> 00:00:43.518
it just puts me in a good mood.

00:00:43.518 --> 00:00:45.718
- This is Mitchell Brand,
I reviewed his chart.

00:00:45.718 --> 00:00:47.582
He's 55 years old, from Chicago,

00:00:47.582 --> 00:00:49.078
divorced with two children,

00:00:49.078 --> 00:00:51.501
you did a radical
prostatectomy on him yesterday.

00:00:51.501 --> 00:00:53.230
- This is actually very accurate.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:54.798
In a post-op unit,

00:00:54.798 --> 00:00:57.492
you will get visited by
the attending physician,

00:00:57.492 --> 00:00:59.672
the head physician, who's
usually the main surgeon.

00:00:59.672 --> 00:01:01.465
They will have their residents
following them along.

00:01:01.465 --> 00:01:04.462
Here, in this case, the patient
has a radical prostatectomy,

00:01:04.462 --> 00:01:07.509
which is a removal of the
entire prostate gland.

00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:08.967
I have a whole video on the prostate

00:01:08.967 --> 00:01:10.847
and how things can go wrong with it,

00:01:10.847 --> 00:01:12.274
so if you wanna check
that out on my channel.

00:01:12.274 --> 00:01:13.982
The thing about the radical prostatectomy,

00:01:13.982 --> 00:01:15.193
why it's a difficult surgery,

00:01:15.193 --> 00:01:17.215
is because there's a lot of nerves in

00:01:17.215 --> 00:01:18.718
the region of the prostate,

00:01:18.718 --> 00:01:19.996
amongst other structures that

00:01:19.996 --> 00:01:21.574
are very anatomically important,

00:01:21.574 --> 00:01:24.147
including your urinary
system, your semen duct.

00:01:24.147 --> 00:01:25.865
Going in to perform that surgery,

00:01:25.865 --> 00:01:28.006
you wanna make sure that
anatomically you're very careful

00:01:28.006 --> 00:01:29.822
not to damage the surrounding structures,

00:01:29.822 --> 00:01:32.134
but as we know, things can
go wrong and things happen.

00:01:32.134 --> 00:01:32.967
- You're late.

00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:33.800
- Five minutes, we've all been there.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:34.878
- Your first day, no less.

00:01:34.878 --> 00:01:36.720
It is your responsibility to be here.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.688
If you were not, you have
failed in your responsibility,

00:01:38.688 --> 00:01:39.783
which makes it your fault.

00:01:39.783 --> 00:01:41.879
- Okay, how can it be my fault?

00:01:41.879 --> 00:01:43.640
I did nothing wrong, the bus.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:44.678
- This is gonna work out great,

00:01:44.678 --> 00:01:47.276
the board clearly made the
right choice in hiring you.

00:01:47.276 --> 00:01:48.109
- Thank you.

00:01:48.109 --> 00:01:49.208
(scoffs)

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:50.823
- I think the attending is being mean,

00:01:50.823 --> 00:01:52.407
but on the other hand, I think he's right.

00:01:52.407 --> 00:01:55.115
As a doctor, it's very
important to be on time.

00:01:55.115 --> 00:01:57.237
There should be no
excuses, because ideally,

00:01:57.237 --> 00:01:58.447
you should be there early.

00:01:58.447 --> 00:02:01.709
Being a good resident, it's
ideal to get places early,

00:02:01.709 --> 00:02:03.344
so that you can review
the charts beforehand,

00:02:03.344 --> 00:02:05.173
get all of the information,
so that when you are

00:02:05.173 --> 00:02:08.111
rounding with your attending,
you know all the information.

00:02:08.111 --> 00:02:10.129
- [Female Doctor] It's
definitely a budding the aorta,

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:11.377
and the left kidney.

00:02:11.377 --> 00:02:13.377
Renal angiomyolipoma?

00:02:13.377 --> 00:02:15.474
- [Male Doctor] You see
an extensive blood supply?

00:02:15.474 --> 00:02:16.439
- [Female Doctor] No.

00:02:16.439 --> 00:02:18.939
- God, I wish I could do this.

00:02:20.049 --> 00:02:22.813
- She has a sarcoma, a malignant tumor.

00:02:22.813 --> 00:02:24.090
(intense music)

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:25.035
- Malignant?

00:02:25.035 --> 00:02:26.219
That means it's killing me, right?

00:02:26.219 --> 00:02:27.052
- Yes!

00:02:27.052 --> 00:02:29.193
- I get that it may have
features of a malignant sarcoma,

00:02:29.193 --> 00:02:32.772
but to know instantly,
without a proper biopsy,

00:02:32.772 --> 00:02:34.696
it could be several things.

00:02:34.696 --> 00:02:36.076
- So that's it?

00:02:36.076 --> 00:02:38.828
You just accept my answer at face value?

00:02:38.828 --> 00:02:39.661
Why?

00:02:40.563 --> 00:02:41.929
- You're very arrogant.

00:02:41.929 --> 00:02:43.682
(laughs)

00:02:43.682 --> 00:02:45.162
Arrogant people don't
think they need to lie.

00:02:45.162 --> 00:02:46.234
- How long will this be?

00:02:46.234 --> 00:02:47.567
- Seven minutes.

00:02:48.586 --> 00:02:49.963
Without any complications,

00:02:49.963 --> 00:02:51.634
it takes seven minutes to do a discharge

00:02:51.634 --> 00:02:53.272
examination properly.

00:02:53.272 --> 00:02:55.216
- Dr. Dunsmear has
already cleared him to go,

00:02:55.216 --> 00:02:56.435
and we need the bed.

00:02:56.435 --> 00:02:59.285
- Okay, protocol requires
that the surgical department

00:02:59.285 --> 00:03:00.209
also clears him.

00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:03.499
- Why is a patient that
has an ear infection

00:03:03.499 --> 00:03:05.443
admitted into the hospital?

00:03:05.443 --> 00:03:08.138
And question number two,
why is a patient that has

00:03:08.138 --> 00:03:10.435
an ear infection being treated by a member

00:03:10.435 --> 00:03:11.557
of the surgical team?

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:12.391
(laughs)

00:03:12.391 --> 00:03:14.216
- What are you doing down here?

00:03:14.216 --> 00:03:15.731
- I'm waiting for her to fart.

00:03:15.731 --> 00:03:18.098
(whimsical music)

00:03:18.098 --> 00:03:20.373
Flatulence, but I'm using the word fart

00:03:20.373 --> 00:03:22.763
in front of the patient to be more casual.

00:03:22.763 --> 00:03:23.596
(laughs)

00:03:23.596 --> 00:03:24.429
You're the president of the hospital,

00:03:24.429 --> 00:03:26.141
so I'll say flatulence to you.

00:03:26.141 --> 00:03:27.587
- Well, I appreciate that.

00:03:27.587 --> 00:03:28.420
(laughs)

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:29.770
Why are you doing that?

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:31.812
- She had her deviated septum repaired,

00:03:31.812 --> 00:03:33.370
before we can release
her, we have to make sure

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:35.459
she isn't suffering from
postoperative ileus.

00:03:35.459 --> 00:03:39.499
- Postoperative ileus is when
the muscles of the intestines

00:03:39.499 --> 00:03:41.101
don't move correctly,

00:03:41.101 --> 00:03:44.323
and it basically, it's
like a frozen intestine,

00:03:44.323 --> 00:03:47.523
meaning that the gut
is not moving the food,

00:03:47.523 --> 00:03:49.755
and its remnants, and
the stool further down,

00:03:49.755 --> 00:03:51.656
and allowing it to pass.

00:03:51.656 --> 00:03:53.496
This can happen sometimes,

00:03:53.496 --> 00:03:55.643
one of the first things you
wait is for the patient to

00:03:55.643 --> 00:03:56.788
pass gas,

00:03:56.788 --> 00:03:57.698
and then in some cases,

00:03:57.698 --> 00:03:58.876
if you had something more complicated,

00:03:58.876 --> 00:04:01.227
like an abdominal surgery,

00:04:01.227 --> 00:04:03.927
you wait for them to have a
stool before sending them home.

00:04:03.927 --> 00:04:04.779
- I think that was it.

00:04:04.779 --> 00:04:07.696
(machines beeping)

00:04:08.705 --> 00:04:09.881
- You don't have to smell it.

00:04:09.881 --> 00:04:11.109
- I don't think so.

00:04:11.109 --> 00:04:12.450
(bouncy piano music)

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:13.660
I'll wait a little longer.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:15.661
This is important.

00:04:15.661 --> 00:04:16.649
- No it's not.

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:19.899
- The procedure is called a laparotomy.

00:04:23.186 --> 00:04:24.451
Your heart sounds good.

00:04:24.451 --> 00:04:26.458
- I don't know what she was
listening to her heart for,

00:04:26.458 --> 00:04:28.666
that was definitely just
an add-on for the show,

00:04:28.666 --> 00:04:30.610
but when we listen to the heart,

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:32.339
we listen in multiple areas.

00:04:32.339 --> 00:04:33.795
When you're listening to one area,

00:04:33.795 --> 00:04:35.115
you're listening to one part of the heart,

00:04:35.115 --> 00:04:36.739
then you move the stethoscope around,

00:04:36.739 --> 00:04:38.491
and you're getting the
other part of the heart,

00:04:38.491 --> 00:04:39.827
which gives you more information.

00:04:39.827 --> 00:04:41.675
Then, you move it to the
other side of the chest,

00:04:41.675 --> 00:04:44.763
where you're listening
to separate arteries,

00:04:44.763 --> 00:04:46.411
so you can hear what the aorta's doing,

00:04:46.411 --> 00:04:48.482
you can hear what the
pulmonary valve is doing.

00:04:48.482 --> 00:04:53.210
- My husband died in a car
accident a couple of years ago.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:55.127
Mark is our only child.

00:04:57.164 --> 00:04:59.452
I can't die right before his wedding.

00:04:59.452 --> 00:05:00.285
I can't.

00:05:02.854 --> 00:05:04.195
- You're not gonna die.

00:05:04.195 --> 00:05:06.731
- Making promises like
that, I've said it before,

00:05:06.731 --> 00:05:07.811
it's not wise.

00:05:07.811 --> 00:05:10.712
We have only so much
control over certain things,

00:05:10.712 --> 00:05:12.796
especially if they don't
know the grade of this tumor,

00:05:12.796 --> 00:05:14.499
they don't know the spread of the tumor.

00:05:14.499 --> 00:05:15.515
- My little girl has a tummy ache

00:05:15.515 --> 00:05:17.721
because mommy and daddy
won't stop fighting.

00:05:17.721 --> 00:05:19.218
This isn't a medical
issue, send them home.

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:20.954
- It could be intestinal malrotation,

00:05:20.954 --> 00:05:22.381
which could quickly become fatal.

00:05:22.381 --> 00:05:25.150
- And every patient in this
hospital could have malaria.

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:26.323
That doesn't mean we're
gonna go around testing

00:05:26.323 --> 00:05:28.227
for every condition we
think they could have.

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:31.235
- Ordering random tests
just to make sure that

00:05:31.235 --> 00:05:33.350
a patient doesn't have that condition

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:34.461
is certainly not smart,

00:05:34.461 --> 00:05:36.414
and I agree with the
senior attending here.

00:05:36.414 --> 00:05:37.663
What you learn from experience is,

00:05:37.663 --> 00:05:41.908
when you go on a hunt and start
ordering a bunch of tests,

00:05:41.908 --> 00:05:43.974
certain tests will come up positive

00:05:43.974 --> 00:05:46.053
mostly because a lot of those tests

00:05:46.053 --> 00:05:50.157
have the high possibility of
coming back as false positive.

00:05:50.157 --> 00:05:52.108
Which then encourages
you to order more tests,

00:05:52.108 --> 00:05:53.855
which has several effects.

00:05:53.855 --> 00:05:55.142
The patient gets anxiety.

00:05:55.142 --> 00:05:57.430
Second, some tests carry risks.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:05:58.263
For example,

00:05:58.263 --> 00:06:00.165
if you start ordering CAT
scans on every patient,

00:06:00.165 --> 00:06:02.792
you're exposing the patient
to unnecessary radiation.

00:06:02.792 --> 00:06:03.712
On top of that,

00:06:03.712 --> 00:06:06.380
you may find on a CAT
scan some kind of nodule,

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:07.836
some kind of node,

00:06:07.836 --> 00:06:09.387
which will need further testing,

00:06:09.387 --> 00:06:12.324
sometimes a biopsy, and
biopsies carry risk.

00:06:12.324 --> 00:06:13.704
You see where I'm going with this?

00:06:13.704 --> 00:06:14.537
- From now on,

00:06:14.537 --> 00:06:16.212
you don't run any tests
you don't have to run.

00:06:16.212 --> 00:06:20.574
- How do I know if a test is
needed until after I run it?

00:06:20.574 --> 00:06:21.948
- She'll tell you.

00:06:21.948 --> 00:06:23.691
- Nurses actually have a lot of experience

00:06:23.691 --> 00:06:26.980
in knowing what test to run,
which tests are excessive,

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:28.588
which tests are very important.

00:06:28.588 --> 00:06:32.694
In fact, when residents in my
hospital ran rapid responses,

00:06:32.694 --> 00:06:35.098
the nurses from the ICU
were so experienced,

00:06:35.098 --> 00:06:36.988
they already knew what
medications to pull up

00:06:36.988 --> 00:06:38.684
at what dosages even more so than

00:06:38.684 --> 00:06:40.100
some of the young residents.

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:42.244
(heart monitor beeps)

00:06:42.244 --> 00:06:44.316
- I'm Dr. Claire Brown,
I'm first assist today,

00:06:44.316 --> 00:06:46.293
and I'll be leading the time out.

00:06:46.293 --> 00:06:47.885
Patient's name?

00:06:47.885 --> 00:06:49.628
- Stephanie Willis.

00:06:49.628 --> 00:06:50.759
- Scheduled surgery?

00:06:50.759 --> 00:06:53.349
- Excision of indeterminate
retroperitoneale tumor.

00:06:53.349 --> 00:06:54.182
- Oh, now it's indeterminate.

00:06:54.182 --> 00:06:56.533
- We don't anticipate any
complications with the surgery.

00:06:56.533 --> 00:06:57.678
- Thank you, Dr. Brown.

00:06:57.678 --> 00:06:59.142
- That time out actually happens,

00:06:59.142 --> 00:07:02.501
it's basically, we confirm
all of the information.

00:07:02.501 --> 00:07:04.284
Generally, we do it while
the patient's awake,

00:07:04.284 --> 00:07:06.252
so they can confirm all
of this information,

00:07:06.252 --> 00:07:08.261
and then we do it one
more time before we start,

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:10.340
including, this may surprise you,

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:13.254
discussing the site of the operation.

00:07:13.254 --> 00:07:15.180
Because it has happened, unfortunately,

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:16.013
horribly,

00:07:16.013 --> 00:07:18.812
that we've operated on the
wrong side of a patient's body.

00:07:18.812 --> 00:07:22.223
There's been a lot of things
procedures put in place

00:07:22.223 --> 00:07:24.533
that prevent us from making
that horrible mistake,

00:07:24.533 --> 00:07:26.524
with the time out being one
of the most important ones.

00:07:26.524 --> 00:07:27.644
- It might be infected.

00:07:27.644 --> 00:07:28.548
- It's not.

00:07:28.548 --> 00:07:30.005
- There is some discoloration.

00:07:30.005 --> 00:07:34.156
- He's 82 years old,
everything is discolored.

00:07:34.156 --> 00:07:36.061
(laughs)

00:07:36.061 --> 00:07:37.995
- Blood pressure 120 over 70,

00:07:37.995 --> 00:07:40.995
heart rate 60, she's holding steady.

00:07:41.941 --> 00:07:43.108
- I have opened the fascia.

00:07:43.108 --> 00:07:44.421
- You know what you guys should do?

00:07:44.421 --> 00:07:46.644
You should screenshot this little scene

00:07:46.644 --> 00:07:48.780
where she says, "I opened the fascia,"

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:51.044
and send it to Gabbie Hannah,

00:07:51.044 --> 00:07:53.314
because she tweeted me not so long ago

00:07:53.314 --> 00:07:55.478
saying that she can't believe that

00:07:55.478 --> 00:07:58.020
after we operate on organs,
and we put them back,

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:00.164
are they just like floating
around in our bodies?

00:08:00.164 --> 00:08:02.177
And I told her that everything
is interconnected with

00:08:02.177 --> 00:08:04.998
fascia, and she had no idea
what fascia looked like,

00:08:04.998 --> 00:08:06.537
so send her this picture on her twitter,

00:08:06.537 --> 00:08:07.370
she's gonna love it.

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:09.621
- I thought you'd wanna see it.

00:08:09.621 --> 00:08:11.269
- It looks like puke.

00:08:11.269 --> 00:08:12.342
- No no no.

00:08:12.342 --> 00:08:14.767
It's not the regular color.

00:08:14.767 --> 00:08:16.086
- Puke has a regular color?

00:08:16.086 --> 00:08:17.260
- Yeah.

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:19.558
- It is a bit of an unusual color.

00:08:19.558 --> 00:08:21.225
We could order some-

00:08:23.423 --> 00:08:25.185
I'm sending you home!

00:08:25.185 --> 00:08:26.262
- Are you sure?

00:08:26.262 --> 00:08:27.095
(laughs)

00:08:27.095 --> 00:08:27.928
- This is so good!

00:08:27.928 --> 00:08:29.745
Part of medicine, and
a big part of medicine,

00:08:29.745 --> 00:08:31.433
and something that I learned
and teach my students

00:08:31.433 --> 00:08:34.602
is that a big part of
what we do is reassure,

00:08:34.602 --> 00:08:37.075
because people worry, and
there's a lot of anxiety that

00:08:37.075 --> 00:08:39.117
comes with people's bodies.

00:08:39.117 --> 00:08:42.207
Fluids start coming out of random places,

00:08:42.207 --> 00:08:43.588
fluids change color,

00:08:43.588 --> 00:08:46.671
we bleed, body aches, our heads hurt,

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:50.412
our vision changes, a cough occurs,

00:08:50.412 --> 00:08:51.511
upset stomach.

00:08:51.511 --> 00:08:53.588
As soon as you go on the
internet and look at any one

00:08:53.588 --> 00:08:54.731
of these websites,

00:08:54.731 --> 00:08:57.050
you could fall down into a wormhole

00:08:57.050 --> 00:08:58.800
thinking that your headache
is caused by a tumor

00:08:58.800 --> 00:08:59.633
like this.

00:08:59.633 --> 00:09:00.466
(snaps)

00:09:00.466 --> 00:09:01.770
- Claire, tell me you got something.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:02.607
- I've got nothing.

00:09:02.607 --> 00:09:06.391
This tumor is way bigger
than it looked on the scans.

00:09:06.391 --> 00:09:07.790
- Hence, why we don't
diagnose people just by

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:09.015
looking at scans.

00:09:09.015 --> 00:09:12.480
- (mumbles) to see her aorta.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:13.995
- That's a problem.

00:09:13.995 --> 00:09:15.598
(machine beeps)

00:09:15.598 --> 00:09:17.448
- The tumor is encasing her aorta,

00:09:17.448 --> 00:09:19.447
meaning that it's fully
surrounding it and engulfing

00:09:19.447 --> 00:09:22.175
the aorta, which serves to
be the main blood supply

00:09:22.175 --> 00:09:23.528
to the bottom of the body.

00:09:23.528 --> 00:09:25.106
- Dr. Melinda sent you a biopsy,

00:09:25.106 --> 00:09:26.736
when will the results be ready?

00:09:26.736 --> 00:09:28.088
- When I get to it.

00:09:28.088 --> 00:09:29.802
- It's very important.

00:09:29.802 --> 00:09:33.024
- They're all very important.

00:09:33.024 --> 00:09:34.992
- Let me see the other test orders.

00:09:34.992 --> 00:09:36.664
I'll tell you which
one's the most important,

00:09:36.664 --> 00:09:38.429
and you can do them in that order.

00:09:38.429 --> 00:09:39.728
I'll be honest and fair.

00:09:39.728 --> 00:09:40.657
(laughs)

00:09:40.657 --> 00:09:42.107
- In complex surgeries like this,

00:09:42.107 --> 00:09:45.128
we not only have the
pathology lab on standby,

00:09:45.128 --> 00:09:47.064
but you could also even have
a pathologist in the room

00:09:47.064 --> 00:09:50.149
with a microscope, ready to
make the call immediately,

00:09:50.149 --> 00:09:53.120
especially when a patient
is under sedation.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:55.592
You don't want to extend the
patient's time under sedation

00:09:55.592 --> 00:09:57.968
just because the pathologist
is busy with other cases.

00:09:57.968 --> 00:10:00.218
- There is one possibility.

00:10:03.344 --> 00:10:04.935
If we remove the left
kidney, we might be able to

00:10:04.935 --> 00:10:07.489
get a good enough view to
successfully remove the mass.

00:10:07.489 --> 00:10:10.896
- Take out a healthy kidney
to get a better view.

00:10:10.896 --> 00:10:11.992
That's insane.

00:10:11.992 --> 00:10:13.462
- The tricky part with this is that

00:10:13.462 --> 00:10:15.657
if they're not successful, she could die.

00:10:15.657 --> 00:10:17.408
If they're somewhat successful,

00:10:17.408 --> 00:10:19.848
she could be incredibly disabled

00:10:19.848 --> 00:10:22.713
and have horrible
disability from the surgery,

00:10:22.713 --> 00:10:25.629
and her last six weeks to
live with this horrible tumor

00:10:25.629 --> 00:10:26.840
are gonna be awful,

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:29.000
as opposed to if they
just suture her up now,

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.214
and she could just enjoy
her last few weeks of life.

00:10:31.214 --> 00:10:33.250
Even if the surgery's a major success,

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:36.456
does that mean they were able
to get the entire tumor out,

00:10:36.456 --> 00:10:37.928
and now she's completely cured?

00:10:37.928 --> 00:10:40.496
It could be spread all
across the peritoneum,

00:10:40.496 --> 00:10:42.771
which is the inside of
the abdominal cavity.

00:10:42.771 --> 00:10:44.159
To me, this sounds too risky

00:10:44.159 --> 00:10:46.046
and too much like a cowboy move,

00:10:46.046 --> 00:10:47.311
but, then again, I'm not a surgeon,

00:10:47.311 --> 00:10:48.263
nor a surgical oncologist.

00:10:48.263 --> 00:10:49.830
That's why I would ask
someone who's more of a

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:52.464
sub-specialist, which I don't
think any of these people are.

00:10:52.464 --> 00:10:53.615
- Am I healthy?

00:10:53.615 --> 00:10:54.448
- Probably.

00:10:54.448 --> 00:10:56.088
- You're a doctor,
you're supposed to know.

00:10:56.088 --> 00:10:58.072
- No we're not, nobody
knows anything for sure.

00:10:58.072 --> 00:11:00.881
Anybody could drop dead of
a heart attack any time.

00:11:00.881 --> 00:11:02.163
- Who is this guy?

00:11:02.163 --> 00:11:03.616
Am I gonna have a heart attack?

00:11:03.616 --> 00:11:05.887
- First of all, don't do this
in an open space like this,

00:11:05.887 --> 00:11:07.332
because if he starts yelling,
it's not gonna look good

00:11:07.332 --> 00:11:08.174
for anybody.

00:11:08.174 --> 00:11:09.585
I have these conversations all the time,

00:11:09.585 --> 00:11:12.302
patients come in and demand
to know that they're healthy,

00:11:12.302 --> 00:11:13.386
there's nothing wrong with them.

00:11:13.386 --> 00:11:14.438
You can't promise that,

00:11:14.438 --> 00:11:16.806
but what I can do is answer
specific questions that

00:11:16.806 --> 00:11:17.804
they have about their health.

00:11:17.804 --> 00:11:20.254
Doctor, my ear hurts,
why do you think that is?

00:11:20.254 --> 00:11:22.077
And I can say, well, I looked in your ear,

00:11:22.077 --> 00:11:23.539
and I found that you
have an ear infection.

00:11:23.539 --> 00:11:25.543
Let's treat it, and see
what the outcome is.

00:11:25.543 --> 00:11:28.160
You give them reassurance
that it's likely to pass,

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:31.168
but you tell them if
the symptoms continue,

00:11:31.168 --> 00:11:32.977
if the symptoms are to worsen,

00:11:32.977 --> 00:11:34.633
if new symptoms appear,

00:11:34.633 --> 00:11:37.396
please return so we can
do a further evaluation.

00:11:37.396 --> 00:11:38.229
That's it.

00:11:38.229 --> 00:11:40.193
- The small bowel is
twisted around the superior

00:11:40.193 --> 00:11:41.559
mesenteric artery.

00:11:41.559 --> 00:11:45.014
Martine needs surgery, immediately.

00:11:45.014 --> 00:11:46.593
- We need to confirm with Dr. Melendez.

00:11:46.593 --> 00:11:48.762
- No, Dr. Melendez is in surgery,

00:11:48.762 --> 00:11:51.545
part of Martine's bowel is
dying, and killing her with it.

00:11:51.545 --> 00:11:53.786
- No, you can not make
these calls on your own.

00:11:53.786 --> 00:11:56.119
Dr. Melendez was very clear.

00:11:57.438 --> 00:11:58.458
- He was very clear.

00:11:58.458 --> 00:11:59.932
It's past midnight, which
means it's tomorrow,

00:11:59.932 --> 00:12:01.580
which means you're no longer my boss.

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:04.826
(heart monitor beeps)

00:12:04.826 --> 00:12:07.514
- Is this the OR scheduler?

00:12:07.514 --> 00:12:09.690
Yes, this is Dr. Murphy.

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:11.204
Prepare an OR for surgery.

00:12:11.204 --> 00:12:15.728
- As feelgood as this is, an
intern cannot just book an OR,

00:12:15.728 --> 00:12:18.154
can't schedule their own surgery,

00:12:18.154 --> 00:12:20.387
no one would ever allow this to happen.

00:12:20.387 --> 00:12:22.566
Actually, this could be
grounds for expulsion

00:12:22.566 --> 00:12:24.566
from a surgical program.

00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:28.711
- Why do all surgeons all
look so frail in these shows?

00:12:28.711 --> 00:12:29.878
Him, McDreamy.

00:12:31.071 --> 00:12:33.897
Man, I get you're operating
all the time, but come on.

00:12:33.897 --> 00:12:36.870
A little deadlift and bench
press never hurt nobody.

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:37.703
- Thank you.

00:12:37.703 --> 00:12:40.286
(upbeat music)

00:12:45.175 --> 00:12:47.568
I like the minimalist
look for an apartment.

00:12:47.568 --> 00:12:49.575
Oh, he got a mattress,
look at that upgrade.

00:12:49.575 --> 00:12:52.456
When I say this show has good writing,

00:12:52.456 --> 00:12:55.015
I'm not talking about the actual speech

00:12:55.015 --> 00:12:56.511
and the words that the characters say,

00:12:56.511 --> 00:12:58.429
that's good, but when I say
they have great writing,

00:12:58.429 --> 00:13:01.528
it means they think
ahead for each situation,

00:13:01.528 --> 00:13:05.029
to make it not only
lighthearted, comical, fun,

00:13:05.029 --> 00:13:08.313
and interesting, but they show
a way to highlight a person

00:13:08.313 --> 00:13:10.903
who has autism in such a unique light

00:13:10.903 --> 00:13:14.135
that it's very positive for people who

00:13:14.135 --> 00:13:15.960
are born or develop this condition.

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:18.759
This is probably one of my
favorite shows right now

00:13:18.759 --> 00:13:20.401
as far as medical dramas go.

00:13:20.401 --> 00:13:21.607
I'm certainly a fan.

00:13:21.607 --> 00:13:22.909
The reason I watched
episode two is because

00:13:22.909 --> 00:13:25.121
I want to learn more
about the first season

00:13:25.121 --> 00:13:27.286
before jumping into the second season.

00:13:27.286 --> 00:13:29.358
Now, if there are some good
episodes within the first season

00:13:29.358 --> 00:13:30.831
you want me to watch, please
let me know down below

00:13:30.831 --> 00:13:32.110
in the comments.

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:34.519
Hit that subscribe button
and the notification bell

00:13:34.519 --> 00:13:35.750
so you get all my videos,

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:37.567
and check out my other content

00:13:37.567 --> 00:13:39.864
if you enjoyed my medical drama reviews.

00:13:39.864 --> 00:13:41.583
You'll love it, or your money back.

00:13:41.583 --> 00:13:43.503
As always, stay happy and healthy.

00:13:43.503 --> 00:13:46.170
(upbeat music)

